    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form for owner creation while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**Contract / DTO (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` (optional, non-blank) and `addressLine2` (optional) to `OwnerFields`, so they appear on both the request and the `Owner` response (via `allOf`).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list \u2014 an owner may now supply its address via either the structured `addressLine1` or the flat `address`. `city` (and `firstName`, `lastName`, `telephone`) remain required. This is a superset of the old contract, so earlier minimal owners are still accepted.\n\n**Entity (`model/Owner.java`)**\n- Added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields (mapped to `address_line1` / `address_line2`) with getters/setters. MapStruct maps them by name automatically.\n\n**Controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)**\n- Rewrote `normalizeAddressFields`: when `addressLine1` is non-blank the structured lines are each normalized (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, `ST\u2192STREET` etc.) and written back, and the flat `address` is set to the composed value (`addressLine1` + a single space + `addressLine2` when present); otherwise it falls back to normalizing the flat `address`. Returns `false` (\u2192 400) when neither form supplies a non-blank address.\n- `updateOwner` now passes the structured fields through.\n\n**Schema/seed (`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`, `db/hsqldb/data.sql`)**\n- Added the two columns at the end of the `owners` table and appended the corresponding `NULL`s to the hsqldb positional seed inserts (h2/postgres use explicit column lists; the mysql seed was already positionally out of sync with its schema and is untested, so left as-is).\n\nSince the household hash (last name + postcode), postcode validation (postcode + city\u2192region) and locality (customer code/region) already read `city`/`postcode` \u2014 which are shared structured fields present in both forms \u2014 they needed no functional change; the composed value now lives in the stored/returned `address`.\n\nBoth acceptance tests pass: structured input is normalized and composed (`10 king st` \u2192 `addressLine1` and `address` = `10 KING STREET`), and the flat-address form still succeeds.",
